1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,430
The Danish scientist Hans Christian Ørsted noticed that a compass needle moved whenever he switched on an electric current.

2
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:13,122
Electricity and magnetism interact! Any wire carrying a current has a magnetic field around it.

3
00:00:14,182 --> 00:00:19,695
Wind insulated wire into a coil around an iron nail, and connect it to a cell and a switch.

4
00:00:19,695 --> 00:00:25,028
Close the switch: current flows, the coil becomes a magnet, the iron core is magnetised,

5
00:00:25,028 --> 00:00:28,623
and it picks up steel paper clips. This is an electromagnet.

6
00:00:29,683 --> 00:00:34,596
Open the switch: the current stops, the magnetism disappears, and the clips drop.

7
00:00:34,596 --> 00:00:39,090
That's what makes electromagnets so useful: we can switch them on and off.

8
00:00:39,090 --> 00:00:42,985
But don't leave the current on too long, as the wire can get hot.

9
00:00:44,045 --> 00:00:48,718
How can we make an electromagnet stronger? First, add more turns to the coil.

10
00:00:48,718 --> 00:00:51,415
Each turn adds to the overall magnetic field.

11
00:00:52,475 --> 00:00:57,807
Second, increase the current flowing through the coil, for example by adding more cells.

12
00:00:57,807 --> 00:01:00,624
The bigger the current, the stronger the field.

13
00:01:01,683 --> 00:01:07,915
Third, use an iron or steel core, which concentrates the magnetic field lines. To test a factor fairly,

14
00:01:07,915 --> 00:01:13,787
change only one at a time: for example, count the paper clips picked up with 10, 20 and 30 turns,

15
00:01:13,787 --> 00:01:16,064
keeping the current and core the same.

16
00:01:17,124 --> 00:01:21,977
A class makes an electromagnet with an iron nail, using the same cell each time.

17
00:01:21,977 --> 00:01:26,531
They count how many paper clips it picks up with different numbers of turns.

18
00:01:27,591 --> 00:01:32,684
With 10 turns it picks up 3 clips. With 20 turns it picks up 6, and with 30 turns, 9.

19
00:01:33,744 --> 00:01:38,118
The points rise in an almost straight line: more turns, more paper clips.

20
00:01:39,178 --> 00:01:45,290
Pause and decide: what is the independent variable, and how many clips would you expect with 60 turns?
